Q21:
Explain the Android Activity lifecycle in detail, including edge cases like multi-window mode and process death. How does the system restore Activity state after process kill?

A:
The Activity lifecycle consists of these main callbacks:

- onCreate(): Called when Activity is first created. Initialize UI and data here.
- onStart(): Activity becomes visible to user.
- onResume(): Activity starts interacting with the user.
- onPause(): Another Activity comes in front but this one is partially visible.
- onStop(): Activity is no longer visible.
- onDestroy(): Activity is destroyed either because user finished it or system reclaimed memory.

Edge Cases:

- Multi-window mode (Android N+): Activity may be visible but not focused, causing multiple lifecycle transitions (onPause/onResume) without onStop/onStart.
- Process death: System may kill app when it is in background to reclaim resources.

State restoration after process kill:

- Android calls onSaveInstanceState(Bundle outState) before stopping the Activity. Save UI state (e.g., input values).
- After process death, system recreates Activity and passes the saved Bundle to onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) and onRestoreInstanceState(Bundle).
- Use this to restore UI state.

Note: Non-UI data should be stored in persistent storage or ViewModel (but ViewModels don’t survive process death).

Code example:

@Override
protected void onSaveInstanceState(Bundle outState) {
    super.onSaveInstanceState(outState);
    outState.putString("user_input", editText.getText().toString());
}

@Override
protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
    setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);
    editText = findViewById(R.id.edit_text);

    if (savedInstanceState != null) {
        String savedInput = savedInstanceState.getString("user_input");
        editText.setText(savedInput);
    }
}

