Q23:
What are memory leaks in Android? Identify common sources and describe strategies/tools to detect and fix them.

A:
A memory leak happens when objects are kept in memory even though they are no longer needed, causing increased memory usage and app crashes.

Common sources:  
- Static references to Activities or Views.  
- Inner classes or anonymous classes holding implicit references to outer classes.  
- Handlers, Threads, or AsyncTasks holding onto Activity references.  
- Bitmaps or large objects not properly recycled.

Detection tools:  
- Android Profiler: Monitors heap usage.  
- LeakCanary: Open-source library that automatically detects leaks during runtime.

Fix strategies:  
- Avoid keeping long-lived references to Context; use ApplicationContext where possible.  
- Use static inner classes and WeakReference for callbacks or Handlers.  
- Unregister listeners and stop background tasks in lifecycle methods.  
- Recycle bitmaps or use Glide/Picasso which manage memory efficiently.

Code example (WeakReference in Handler):

static class MyHandler extends Handler {
    private final WeakReference<MyActivity> activityRef;

    MyHandler(MyActivity activity) {
        activityRef = new WeakReference<>(activity);
    }

    @Override
    public void handleMessage(Message msg) {
        MyActivity activity = activityRef.get();
        if (activity != null) {
            // Process message
        }
    }
}

